          {
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-10-16T20:14:14Z",
            "text": "In a C locale, chartr() can read past a heap allocation when a UTF-8 replacement character cannot be represented in the native encoding. The OSS-Fuzz testcase below reaches this path; a smaller reproducer is:\n\nReproducible example:\n\nSys.setlocale(\"LC_CTYPE\", \"C\")\nchartr(\"a\", intToUtf8(256L), \"a\")\n\nObserved on R-devel r90684: an AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow read in mkCharCE() (READ of size 3). Expected: an ordinary R error indicating that the translated string cannot be represented in the current locale.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nIn do_chartr() (src/main/character.c), wcstombs(NULL, wc, 0) returns (size_t)-1 when the translated output cannot be encoded in the native locale. The code casts this to int -1 and passes it to CallocCharBuf(). The subsequent wcstombs() call has no room to write a terminating NUL; markKnown() then reaches mkCharCE(), which reads past the buffer while finding its length.\n\nProposed patch: attached as chartr-native-conversion.diff. It retains the size_t byte count, checks for conversion failure and the R string-length limit before allocating, and adds a C-locale regression test. The patch applies with patch -p0 against trunk r90684. In an ASan build, the smaller reproducer and an R-level replay of the 267-byte OSS-Fuzz input now raise an ordinary R error; reg-tests-1a.Rout passes.\n\nOSS-Fuzz testcase: https://oss-fuzz.com/testcase-detail/5327307326423040\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/383, where CI results can be seen.",
